## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory ordering, lock-free data structures, and weak memory models in OS CPUs. It probes ability to identify a real race and reason about inter-thread visibility across architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free synchronization
- memory_order_release
- memory_order_acquire
- Data visibility before signaling availability

## Code Example

```cpp
// Pseudo-C++ lock-free sketch
std::atomic<size_t> head{0}, tail{0};
int data[N];

void produce(int v){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  data[t % N] = v;
  tail.store((t+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
}

int consume(){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  auto h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (h == t) return -1;
  int v = data[h % N];
  head.store((h+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
  return v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this pattern for multiple producers/consumers?
- What tests would you add to validate correctness across ARM/x86?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests IPC basics, race-free data sharing, and practical use of CAS and memory barriers in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free synchronization
- Atomic operations and memory ordering
- Ring buffer wraparound
- Empty/full conditions and visibility

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_uint head, tail; Message buf[N]; } Channel;

bool send(Channel *c, Message m){
  unsigned long t = atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  unsigned long w = (t + 1) % N;
  if (w == atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // full
  while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->tail, &t, w, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  c->buf[t] = m;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool recv(Channel *c, Message *out){
  unsigned long h;
  do {
    h = atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    if (h == atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->head, &h, (h+1)%N, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  *out = c->buf[h];
  return true;
}
```

